      ":question/id": "6b6ec097-b6d5-5784-87e4-5077b4b39769",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-69937",
      ":question/problem": "The average gas price in Chicago is $\\$3.11$ per gallon. If gas prices have been *decreasing* continuously at a rate of $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ per month, what was gas price $5$ months ago? Round your answer to the nearest cent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": true,
      ":question/worked-solution": "The continuously compounded growth equation is $P=P_0e^{rt}$. Here $P=3.11$, $r=-0.01$ per month, and $t=5$ months. Solve for the earlier gas price $P_0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.11 &= P_0e^{(-0.01)(5)} \\\\\nP_0 &= \\frac{3.11}{e^{-0.05}} \\\\\n&\\approx 3.269453.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest cent, the earlier price was $\\$3.27$ per gallon."
    },
