      ":question/id": "7f5f8698-9a93-5720-8b1d-1aedf9ddca22",
      ":question/problem": "The average gas price in Chicago is $\\$2.23$ per gallon. Gas prices have been *decreasing* continuously at a rate of $2.2\\%$ per month. What was the gas price $6$ months ago? Round your answer to the nearest cent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": true,
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the continuously compounded growth equation $P=P_0e^{rt}$. The current amount is $P=2.23$, the rate is $r=-0.022$ (negative for decay), and the elapsed time is $t=6$ months. The time unit matches the rate. Substitute and divide by the exponential factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2.23 &= P_0e^{(-0.022)(6)} \\\\\n2.23 &= P_0e^{-0.132} \\\\\ne^{-0.132} &\\approx 0.87634100 \\\\\nP_0 &= \\frac{2.23}{e^{-0.132}} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe earlier gas price was $\\$2.54$ per gallon. Round only the final amount; keep full calculator precision in the exponential factor."
    },
