      ":question/problem": "The average gas price in Chicago is $\\$4.96$ per gallon. Gas prices have been *growing* continuously at a rate of $1.1\\%$ per month. What was the gas price $4$ months ago? Round your answer to the nearest cent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": true,
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the continuously compounded growth equation $P=P_0e^{rt}$. The current amount is $P=4.96$, the rate is $r=0.011$ (positive for growth), and the elapsed time is $t=4$ months. The time unit matches the rate. Substitute and divide by the exponential factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.96 &= P_0e^{(0.011)(4)} \\\\\n4.96 &= P_0e^{0.044} \\\\\ne^{0.044} &\\approx 1.04498235 \\\\\nP_0 &= \\frac{4.96}{e^{0.044}} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe earlier gas price was $\\$4.75$ per gallon. Round only the final amount; keep full calculator precision in the exponential factor."
    }
  ],
  ":knowledge-point/title": "Calculating an Initial Amount"
}
